ars you  know we've had hearings on  radicalization on some of these social  media platforms there have been concerns  about political bias and they're in  their news gathering services obviously  the election meddling by the Russians is  another issue so I think all these  things have sort of come to a head and  it's time for Congress to exercise some  oversight will determine after we do  that whether or not there are steps that  need to be taken in the form of  regulation or legislation going forward  but right now we want to get the answers  and we think Facebook needs to be  accountable and  parent and I'm glad that mr. Zuckerberg  is appearing today and this is a rare  issue that has united both sides of the  aisle in Congress with concern does it  surprise you that the president has been  virtually radio silent on this issue you  know I'm not sure that it's in his  domain at the moment I suspect that you  know right now it's sort of a  congressional interest and I think it's  part of our oversight responsibilities  the committee that I chair has oversight  of the Federal Trade Commission which is  responsible for consumer protection and  management of privacy he speaks about  other tech companies like Amazon right  well he does he does seem to have an  interest in Amazon and I think I suspect  at some point that we'll hear from the  President on this issue as well but I  think the whole issue of social media  and its influence in our lives and and  the data they have access to is going to  be an ongoing issue now because more  Americans are concerned about their  privacy and I think they have a reason  to be senator we've already seen  Facebook change its tune on supporting  that it will verify the identity of  political ads and its creators that are  on Facebook however when it comes to  foreign influence in our elections has  facebook done enough what concerns you  well I think Norah that they they're  doing they're gonna he's gonna lay out a  number of steps today that they've taken  since the 2016 election and they are  they're taking a lot of sort of self  pollute policing moves I guess I would  say in terms of trying to root out that  kind of influence and I think their  platforms have become a lot more a lot  cleaner and I think they've got  algorithms now that they can use and  artificial intelligence and different  types of mechanisms to to clean and  clear that up so I think they'll be much  better at it going forward and obviously  this is sort of a wake-up call to them  that they're going to have to senator  who else would you like to hear from in  the coming weeks and months ranking  member Nelson has suggested bringing in  Cambridge analytic Oh would you do that  I I expect at some point we will you  know obviously we're interested in other  firms too that may have had access to  the data in the same way that Cambridge  analytic I did they just announced over  the weekend say Facebook did that  they've suspended yet another firm an  analytics firm and so there are we're  gonna try and get to the bottom of this  and I think it's  entirely likely at some point in the  future that Cambridge analytics and  perhaps other firms may be called in to  to talk about how they got access to  this data how they used it and whether  or not when they were told to they shut  it down I mean some of these firms  violated obviously the user agreements  that they had or the contracts that they  had with Facebook and that too is a big  problem that we need to we need to  address all right senator Thune thanks  so much good luck today
 

